I wrote nothing, because `--probe` isn't a date.

`bin/nightly-diary.sh:16` takes the day from its first argument, `DATE=${1:-$(date -v-1d +%F)}`. Something ran it with `--probe` as that argument. `logs/--probe.log` shows the run: "product diary for --probe — started 2026-10-05 09:45". It looks like a test of the runner or wrapper.

The real previous day is 2026-10-04. I couldn't tell whether that is the day you meant, so I didn't scan any projects.

`posts/--probe/index.md` would be a junk "nothing shipped" entry, and the runner would publish it. A project scan against a bogus date would be just as meaningless.

To avoid this, have `nightly-diary.sh` reject any `$1` that isn't `YYYY-MM-DD`. To write 2026-10-04, rerun the runner with that date.

Also from the log: the runner's rebase step failed with `fatal: invalid upstream 'FETCH_HEAD'`. It continued on the local state.
